--TEST--
A comment is trivia in all three spellings, it may sit between any two tokens, a block comment ends at its first `*/` and never nests, and exactly three slashes is a doc comment while four or more is an ordinary one (`rule:tooling/doc-comment-is-three-slashes`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/comments
// A `//` comment runs to the end of the line, on a line of its own or after
// code.
echo "first", "\n"; // trailing

# A `#` comment is the same thing spelled differently.
echo "second", "\n"; # trailing

/* A block comment covers any span, including part of one line. */
echo /* between two tokens */ "third", "\n";

/* It may also
   run over several lines. */

// `rule:tooling/doc-comment-is-three-slashes`: exactly three slashes is a doc
// comment, and four or more is an ordinary comment -- a divider is not
// documentation. Neither is visible to the program.
/// Documents the class under it.
class Greeter {
    //// ------------------------------------------------------------
    //// An ordinary comment, four slashes wide.
    //// ------------------------------------------------------------

    /// Documents the method under it.
    public static function greet(string $who): string {
        return "hello, " . $who;
    }
}

echo Greeter::greet("world"), "\n";

// A block comment ends at its first `*/`, so a second `/*` inside one opens
// nothing and the statement after it is code.
/* outer /* inner */ echo "fourth", "\n";

// Outside a comment the same characters are ordinary text: what is between
// the quotes is the value, marker or not.
echo "a // and a /* and a #", "\n";

// The line is what ends a line comment, so a statement below one still runs.
int $n = 0; // set here
$n = $n + 7; # added here
echo $n, "\n";
--EXPECT--
first
second
third
hello, world
fourth
a // and a /* and a #
7
